1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Dump, restore, verify. A backup matters only when recovery works.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,500
Someone exports each table as a CSV, one file at a time.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Between the two downloads, the application keeps writing.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:15,500
The files capture different moments: an event whose parent is missing.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
And CSV rows carry no keys, checks, indexes, or grants: rows without rules.

6
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,100
A logical dump rebuilds the meaning of objects, not a byte copy of storage files.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,600
Scope is a choice: this schema only. Roles and other project services are not in it.

8
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,800
The archive's table of contents: the expected objects appear.

9
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,100
A checksum detects later changes to the bytes, compared with a trusted earlier hash.

10
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,800
Neither proves PostgreSQL can restore it. And an untrusted dump can run SQL.

11
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:50,100
First create a different, empty database just for this drill.

12
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:52,400
The source is never overwritten. If the restore fails, the original is still there.

13
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,300
--exit-on-error stops at the first error; --single-transaction leaves nothing half-restored.

14
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,200
--no-owner and --no-privileges skip owners and grants: access must be reviewed separately.

15
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:00,900
Both databases share one temporary server here. Real backups need an independent location.

16
00:01:05,266 --> 00:01:06,766
Structure, counts, a relationship check, and a grouped report all match.

17
00:01:07,266 --> 00:01:10,966
But suppose the restore changed ticket 1001's subject. Every check above still passes.

18
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:14,966
A known-record check catches it: same counts, different meaning.

19
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:17,966
Behavior: does the restored status rule still reject a bad value?

20
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:21,266
Pass only on the check-constraint code and the exact constraint name.

21
00:01:21,766 --> 00:01:23,766
Any error is not a test. ROLLBACK keeps the data clean either way.

22
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:35,366
Remember: pg_dump exports related tables from one consistent snapshot. A list or checksum describes the file. Only a restore tests it. Restore into a separate, empty target. Never over the only copy. Counts, a known record, and a constraint test catch different failures. Say what you did not check: roles, grants, the app connection.

